The user is asking why there's a mock mode at all - they see it as redundant/unnecessary. Let me check how the mock mode works in this project to give an accurate answer.

From what I've seen in client.ts, the mock mode is automatically activated when the backend is unreachable:

```typescript
if ((error.code === 'ERR_NETWORK' || !error.response) && localStorage.getItem('use_mock') !== 'true') {
  // retry once, then:
  localStorage.setItem('use_mock', 'true');
  window.dispatchEvent(new Event('mock-mode-changed'));
  window.location.reload();
}
```

So it's an automatic fallback, not something the user explicitly enables. When the backend is down, the frontend automatically switches to mock mode so you can still develop and test the UI.

The user's point is valid - for a feature like avatar upload, the mock mode can't really simulate file storage. Let me explain the purpose and the tradeoff honestly.

